  "doc.title": "How Chromium Renders a Web Page · A Hands-on Essay",
  "doc.desc": "Run one line of code—el.style.transform = 'translateX(120px)'—and it glides cleanly at 120 Hz even when main-thread JavaScript stalls for 200 ms. Animate el.style.left instead, or tuck a moving badge behind overlapping cards, and frames drop or VRAM spikes. Tracing Chromium's RenderingNG pipeline (Blink + cc + Viz, 2026 architecture), this essay follows a byte stream all the way to display swap: multi-process isolation, HTML tokenization and preload scanning, right-to-left selector matching and InvalidationSets, LayoutNG immutable fragments and forced synchronous layout, the four Property Trees, PaintRecords and layer explosion, TileManager tiling and checkerboarding, Viz SurfaceAggregator across OOPIFs, compositor animation isolation, and the November 2026 frontier across Skia Graphite and Speculation Rules.",
