  "ch2.read.dcl": "DOMContentLoaded",
  "ch2.read.blk": "Parser blocked",
  "ch2.read.pre": "Preload saved",
  "ch2.say.blockingCssWait": "JS finished downloading at {jsf} ms, yet the blocking <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> had to wait until purple CSSOM completed ({cssEnd} ms) before executing; HTML paused <em>{blk} ms</em> (DCL: {dcl} ms).",
  "ch2.say.blockingJsWait": "Synchronous <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> froze the HTML parser for <em>{blk} ms</em> until fetch and execution ended at {jsEnd} ms; preload scanner saved {pre} ms (DCL: {dcl} ms).",
  "ch2.say.deferReady": "With <code>defer</code> / <code>type=\"module\"</code>, HTML parsing never paused ({blk} ms blocked); First Paint fired early at <em>{fp} ms</em> while the script ran after DOM + CSSOM at {dcl} ms.",
  "ch2.say.asyncEarly": "The <code>async</code> script arrived early at {jsf} ms and preempted HTML tokenization only while executing for <em>{blk} ms</em> (DCL: {dcl} ms).",
  "ch2.say.asyncAfter": "The <code>async</code> script finished downloading after HTML completed ({htmlEnd} ms), causing <em>{blk} ms</em> parser blocking (First Paint: {fp} ms).",
  "ch2.p4": "At the defaults (30 ms external CSS, 70 ms JS split between fetch and execution), turning <code>HTMLPreloadScanner</code> on lets <code>app.js</code> start downloading at 2 ms alongside the stylesheet, finishing <code>DOMContentLoaded</code> at 84 ms. Uncheck the preload scanner and the parser does not even discover <code>app.js</code> until 16 ms, pushing completion out to 98 ms—costing 14 ms of idle wait. Switch the mode to <code>defer</code> and HTML tokenization races straight past without pausing, pulling First Paint down to 32 ms even though the script finishes running at 72 ms. Drag CSS time above 50 ms to watch even an already-downloaded blocking script wait idly until purple CSSOM completes.",
  "ch2.limit": "The model omits: TCP/TLS connection setup, incremental UTF-8 decoder chunking, legacy <code>document.write()</code> parser re-entry, and non-blocking conditional stylesheets such as <code>media=\"print\"</code>.",
  "ch3.title": "Style calculation: right-to-left matching & InvalidationSets",
  "toc.ch3": "Style matching & invalidation",
  "ch3.law": "Blink evaluates CSS selectors <strong>from right to left</strong>, starting with the rightmost key selector; an ancestor Bloom filter (<code>SelectorFilter</code>) rejects impossible ancestor chains in O(1), and precompiled <code>InvalidationSet</code> bitmasks restrict DOM class toggles to the affected subtree.",
  "ch3.p1": "If you need to find everyone in a city directory whose last name is \"Chen\", who lives in District 4, and whose grandfather taught at East High, you do not pull every graduate roster of East High first. You jump straight to the \"Chen\" section (the rightmost constraint), filter District 4, and only then check family history.",
  "ch3.p2": "That is why Blink's <code>SelectorChecker</code> reads <code>body div.app main ul li a.btn</code> <strong>right to left</strong>. Starting at <code>body</code> would fan out across thousands of irrelevant branches. Instead, Blink hashes every rule into <code>RuleHash</code> buckets keyed by its rightmost key selector (<code>.btn</code>, <code>span</code>, or the fallback universal bucket <code>*</code>). A node only inspects buckets matching its own tag, ID, and classes.",
  "dg.style.cap": "Figure 3. Blink style matching and invalidation narrowing. Rules are bucketed by rightmost key selector, pruned in O(1) via SelectorFilter, and targeted via InvalidationSet on class toggles.",
  "dg.style.aria": "Diagram: four-stage funnel for right-to-left CSS matching using RuleHash buckets, Bloom filter rejection, ancestor walk, and InvalidationSet targeting",
  "dg.style.sel": "body div.app main ul li a.btn",
  "dg.style.sels": "← Match starts at rightmost key a.btn",
  "dg.style.s1": "1. RuleHash Key Buckets",
  "dg.style.s1s": "Inspect only matching tag, id, or class",
  "dg.style.s2": "2. SelectorFilter (Bloom Filter)",
  "dg.style.s2s": "One bitmask skips 90%+ dead ancestors",
  "dg.style.s3": "3. Parent Walk & InvalidationSet",
  "dg.style.s3s": "Walk up on pass; toggle dirties subtree",
  "ch3.p3": "Even after a key selector hits, walking up every element's parent chain would still be expensive. Blink keeps a compact bitmask Bloom filter of every element's ancestor tags, IDs, and classes inside <code>SelectorFilter</code>. Before walking upward, Blink checks the ancestor bitmask: if the Bloom filter says an ancestor class is absent, it <strong>cannot exist</strong> (zero false negatives), so Blink drops the rule immediately. And when JavaScript calls <code>card.classList.toggle('is-open')</code>, <code>StyleEngine</code> consults stylesheet-compiled <code>InvalidationSet</code> masks so only <code>NeedsStyleRecalc</code> nodes inside the impacted subtree recompute styles.",
  "ch3.model.title": "Model 2 · Selector matching, Bloom filter & InvalidationSet cost",
  "ch3.model.how": "Pick a selector pattern (simple class / deep descendant / universal tail), drag DOM size and rule count, and uncheck optimizations to see full-tree style recalculation blow up.",
