  "ch3.say.smartSimple": "Simple class <code>.btn</code> hashes straight into one RuleHash bucket: only <em>{inv}</em> dirty nodes, {chk} key checks, and 0 ancestor walks ({ms} ms).",
  "ch3.say.smartDeep": "Even for a long descendant selector, right-to-left matching plus the Bloom filter rejected <em>{blm}</em> dead paths in O(1), recalculating {inv} nodes across {chk} key checks ({ms} ms).",
  "ch3.say.smartUniversal": "Trap: <code>.card *</code> falls into the universal bucket! Even with only {inv} dirty nodes, key checks jump to <em>{chk}</em> and parent hops climb to {walk} ({ms} ms).",
  "ch3.say.naiveFull": "Without InvalidationSet and the Bloom filter, toggling one card class dirties all <em>{inv}</em> DOM nodes, driving {chk} key checks and {walk} ancestor walks ({ms} ms).",
  "ch3.p4": "In the default 180-node DOM tree with 250 rules, toggling <code>.is-open</code> on one card with Blink's smart engine dirties only 5 nodes; with deep descendant rules (<code>body div.app ... a.btn</code>), it executes 125 key checks and rejects 25 ancestor queries in O(1) via the Bloom filter. Switch to a universal-tail rule like <code>.card <em></code>: because <code></em></code> lands in the universal bucket, even 5 dirty nodes trigger 950 rule checks and 2,275 parent hops! Uncheck the optimization toggle to watch a naive engine re-evaluate all 180 nodes across 45,000 checks.",
  "ch3.limit": "The model omits: CSS cascade specificity ordering, inherited custom property <code>var(--x)</code> propagation, upward invalidation via <code>:has()</code>, and Shadow DOM style scoping boundaries.",
  "ch4.title": "LayoutNG & forced synchronous layout thrashing",
  "toc.ch4": "LayoutNG & layout thrashing",
  "ch4.law": "LayoutNG turns box geometry into a pure function from an immutable <code>ConstraintSpace</code> input to an immutable <code>PhysicalBoxFragment</code> output so clean subtrees hit cache; reading <code>offsetHeight</code> immediately after mutating <code>style.width</code> forces Blink to run Style and Layout synchronously mid-script, turning one end-of-frame pass into N forced reflows.",
  "ch4.p1": "Suppose a carpentry crew plans to measure every room once at 5 PM and cut all trim boards in one batch. Instead, after nailing a single piece of molding in the living room, you shout \"Stop everything and re-measure the hallway!\"—fifty times in a loop. One walk through the house becomes fifty full-house interruptions.",
  "ch4.p2": "Chromium's legacy layout engine mutated shared fields in place across floating and inline trees, making caching brittle. <strong>LayoutNG</strong> rebuilt layout around immutable data boundaries: a parent passes down a <strong><code>ConstraintSpace</code></strong> (available inline/block size, BFC formatting flags, writing mode), and <code>BlockLayoutAlgorithm</code> or <code>FlexLayoutAlgorithm</code> returns a <code>LayoutResult</code> holding physical coordinates inside an immutable <strong><code>PhysicalBoxFragment</code></strong> tree. Any clean subtree seeing the same <code>ConstraintSpace</code> reuses its cached fragment outright.",
  "dg.layoutng.cap": "Figure 4. LayoutNG's immutable input/output contract, contrasted with batched end-of-frame layout versus interleaved read-write Layout Thrashing.",
  "dg.layoutng.aria": "Diagram: ConstraintSpace feeding PhysicalBoxFragment, followed by Batched 1-pass layout vs Forced Synchronous Layout Thrashing",
  "dg.layoutng.cs": "ConstraintSpace (Immutable Input)",
  "dg.layoutng.css": "Available width/height, BFC, writing mode",
  "dg.layoutng.frag": "PhysicalBoxFragment (Immutable Output)",
  "dg.layoutng.frags": "Fixed offsets; clean subtrees reuse cache",
  "dg.layoutng.bat": "Batched: read all offsets, then write widths",
  "dg.layoutng.bats": "Single Style + LayoutNG pass at frame end",
  "dg.layoutng.thr": "Layout Thrashing (Forced Reflow)",
  "dg.layoutng.thrs": "Write width + read offsetHeight → N reflows",
  "ch4.p3": "Normally, writing <code>el.style.width = '360px'</code> merely sets a dirty bit—Blink defers layout until the end of the frame. Yet if the very next statement reads a layout-dependent metric such as <code>offsetHeight</code> or <code>getBoundingClientRect()</code>, Blink has no choice but to flush pending Style and LayoutNG synchronously on the main thread before returning the number. Repeating write-then-read inside a loop creates <strong>Forced Synchronous Layout</strong>—better known as <strong>Layout Thrashing</strong>.",
  "ch4.model.title": "Model 3 · LayoutNG batching vs. Forced Synchronous Layout thrashing",
  "ch4.model.how": "Compare three loop patterns (interleaved write-then-read / batched read-then-write / CSS contain: layout isolation), drag card and tree counts, and watch layout work cross 60 Hz and 120 Hz limits.",
  "ch4.ctl.pat": "Loop pattern",
