  "ch4.cv.b120": "120 Hz budget (8.33 ms)",
  "ch4.cv.x": "Time (ms)",
  "ch4.read.ref": "Forced reflows",
  "ch4.read.ms": "Total layout work",
  "ch4.read.b60": "60 Hz budget share",
  "ch4.read.b120": "120 Hz budget share",
  "ch4.say.thrashOver60": "Interleaved writes and reads forced <em>{ref} synchronous reflows</em> totaling <em>{ms} ms</em>—layout alone consumes {b60}% of a 60 Hz frame budget and drops frames!",
  "ch4.say.thrashOver120": "Write-then-read thrashing fired {ref} reflows (<em>{ms} ms</em>), fitting inside 60 Hz yet swallowing {b120}% of a 120 Hz frame budget.",
  "ch4.say.thrashFits": "At this small scale {ref} forced reflows take {ms} ms within budget, though cost scales quadratically as items or DOM depth grow.",
  "ch4.say.batchedClean": "Batching reads before writes eliminates forced reflows (<em>0</em>); one deferred LayoutNG pass takes just {ms} ms ({b60}% of 60 Hz).",
  "ch4.say.containedFast": "CSS <code>contain: layout</code> isolates each reflow to one card subtree: even with {ref} forced reads, total layout drops to <em>{ms} ms</em> ({b60}% of 60 Hz).",
  "ch4.p4": "Across 50 cards inside an uncontained 300-node tree, alternating write-then-read fires 50 forced reflows totaling 17.55 ms—layout alone consumes 105.3% of a 60 Hz frame budget (and more than double a 120 Hz frame!). Flip to the batched pattern (\"read all <code>offsetHeight</code> values first, write all widths second\"): forced reflows drop to zero, and the single end-of-frame LayoutNG pass takes just 0.54 ms—32.5× faster. Even if legacy scripts cannot reorder the loop, adding CSS <code>contain: layout</code> locks each reflow to one isolated card subtree, bringing work down to 1.29 ms.",
  "ch4.limit": "The model omits: HarfBuzz inline text shaping inside <code>InlineLayoutAlgorithm</code>, multi-pass flex/grid sizing fallbacks, and device-specific microsecond timings (parameterized here at 1.8 µs per visited node).",
  "ch5.title": "Pre-Paint & the four Property Trees",
  "toc.ch5": "Pre-Paint & Property Trees",
  "ch5.law": "In Pre-Paint, Blink extracts transforms, clips, visual effects, and scroll state out of the box hierarchy into four separate <strong>Property Trees</strong>; updating a <code>transform</code> or scrolling a list edits one node on those trees in <code>cc</code>, letting Chromium skip Layout and Paint completely.",
  "ch5.p1": "Think of a slide projector. If the artwork on the glass slide hasn't changed and you only want to shift the picture three centimeters right, dim its brightness, or pan across a window mask, you do not send the slide back to an illustrator to redraw (Paint) or re-typeset the text (Layout)—you just turn one knob on the lens barrel.",
  "ch5.p2": "Older browsers coupled geometry, overflow clipping, and opacity onto one monolithic layer hierarchy, forcing layer splits whenever nested boxes diverged. In RenderingNG's <strong>Pre-Paint</strong> pass, <code>PaintPropertyTreeBuilder</code> constructs four lightweight, orthogonal trees:",
  "ch5.pt1": "<strong>Transform Tree</strong>: CSS transforms (<code>translate</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>rotate</code>) and scroll offsets;",
  "ch5.pt2": "<strong>Clip Tree</strong>: <code>overflow: hidden</code> boxes, <code>clip-path</code> bounds, and viewport rects;",
  "ch5.pt3": "<strong>Effect Tree</strong>: <code>opacity</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>backdrop-filter</code>, and blend modes;",
  "ch5.pt4": "<strong>Scroll Tree</strong>: scroll container bounds, <code>scrollTop / scrollLeft</code> offsets, and scroll chaining.",
  "dg.proptrees.cap": "Figure 5. The four RenderingNG Property Trees built during Pre-Paint. Each PaintChunk carries just four node pointers rather than duplicating ancestor state.",
  "dg.proptrees.aria": "Diagram: Transform, Clip, Effect, and Scroll property trees feeding a PaintChunk holding four state pointers",
  "dg.proptrees.tr": "Transform Tree",
  "dg.proptrees.trs": "Local matrix × scroll translation",
