  "ch9.cv.aria": "Top plot shows per-frame cost against the VSync budget line; bottom compares smooth motion against dropped-frame stalls",
  "ch9.cv.thresh": "Budget {v} ms",
  "ch9.cv.ms": "Frame time (ms)",
  "ch9.cv.drop": "Long-task stall",
  "ch9.cv.box": "Motion track",
  "ch9.cv.x": "Window (seconds)",
  "ch9.read.cost": "Base frame cost",
  "ch9.read.drop": "Dropped frames (1.6 s)",
  "ch9.read.max": "Longest freeze",
  "ch9.read.fps": "Effective FPS",
  "ch9.say.compositorImmune": "Compositor-driven <code>transform</code> runs just 1 stage on <code>cc</code> ({cost} ms): despite {js} ms JS spikes on Main, it drops <em>0 frames</em> and holds {fps} fps!",
  "ch9.say.paintStalled": "<code>box-shadow</code> skips LayoutNG but still runs 3 main-thread stages (base {cost} ms), hitting {js} ms JS stalls to drop <em>{drop} frames</em> (max freeze {max} ms).",
  "ch9.say.layoutStalled": "Mutating <code>left</code> reruns all 5 stages every frame (base {cost} ms) plus {js} ms JS spikes, dropping <em>{drop} frames</em> and collapsing output to {fps} fps (max pause {max} ms)!",
  "ch9.say.cleanMain": "With {js} ms JS spikes and light DOM ({cost} ms base), main-thread animation fits inside {bud} ms ({drop} dropped). Push the JS spike or DOM slider right!",
  "ch9.p3": "Under a 120 Hz screen with 250 boxes and periodic 50 ms main-thread JS spikes: compositor-driven <code>transform</code> drops 0 frames across the entire 1.6-second run, locked at 120 fps. Animating <code>box-shadow</code> loses 12 frames behind paint + JS stalls, while animating <code>left</code> drops 102 frames with a 60.5 ms freeze—collapsing effective output down to 56.3 fps.",
  "ch9.limit": "The model omits a crucial pitfall: updating <code>el.style.transform</code> manually inside a JavaScript <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> loop. Even though <code>transform</code> still skips Layout and Paint, the <code>rAF</code> callback itself runs on the main thread, so a 50 ms JS stall still freezes it—only declarative CSS animations, transitions, or Web Animations API animations offloaded to <code>cc</code> gain true main-thread immunity.",
  "ch10.title": "Where the frontier is (November 2026)",
  "toc.ch10": "Where the frontier is (Nov 2026)",
  "ch10.law": "Chromium's 2026 rendering frontier pushes three shifts in parallel: replacing single-threaded OpenGL-era Ganesh with multi-threaded WebGPU/Dawn-backed <strong>Skia Graphite</strong>, moving scroll-linked and page-transition animations onto the compositor thread, and running the entire Blink pipeline ahead of navigation via <strong>Speculation Rules</strong>.",
  "ch10.p1": "Below, facts as of November 2026 are separated into established pipeline invariants, shipped engineering capabilities with release dates, and active boundaries or trials.",
  "ch10.h.est": "Established",
  "ch10.e1": "<strong>RenderingNG multi-process & multi-thread split</strong>: Immutable LayoutNG fragments, the four Pre-Paint Property Trees, <code>PaintArtifactCompositor</code>, and <code>Viz::SurfaceAggregator</code> remain Chromium's unified architecture across desktop and mobile within strict 16.67 ms (60 Hz) and 8.33 ms (120 Hz) display budgets.",
  "ch10.e2": "<strong>Main-thread vs. compositor boundary</strong>: Any property or callback executing on Blink's main event loop remains vulnerable to JavaScript long tasks; true jank isolation requires keeping per-frame mutations inside compositor property trees.",
  "ch10.h.demo": "Already built",
  "ch10.d1": "<strong>Skia Graphite on Dawn (WebGPU abstraction)</strong>: Chromium's next-generation Skia backend replaces the OpenGL-centric single-threaded Ganesh backend with <strong>Graphite</strong> layered on <strong>Dawn</strong> (mapping natively to <strong>Metal</strong> on macOS, <strong>Direct3D 12</strong> on Windows, and <strong>Vulkan</strong> on Android/Linux). Graphite splits command recording across multiple parallel <code>Recorder</code> threads and performs 2D depth testing to drop hidden overdraw early. Google began shipping Graphite in production on Apple Silicon Macs around <strong>Chrome 135 (April 2025)</strong>—reporting roughly <strong>~15% MotionMark 1.3</strong> gains alongside lower GPU process memory (Chrome blog, 2025–2026)—before expanding rollout across desktop milestones 136–140+.",
  "ch10.d2": "<strong>Compositor-thread Scroll-Driven Animations</strong>: Shipped in <strong>Chrome 115+</strong> and broadly supported across modern browsers by 2025–2026 (including <code>Animation.overallProgress</code> in Chrome 133), CSS <code>animation-timeline: scroll()</code> and <code>view()</code> bind progress straight to <code>cc::ScrollTree</code> offsets, eliminating main-thread JS scroll listeners for parallax and reading indicators.",
  "ch10.d3": "<strong>Same-document & Cross-document View Transitions</strong>: Same-origin MPA transitions (<code>@view-transition &#123; navigation: auto; &#125;</code>, shipped in <strong>Chrome 126</strong>) capture compositor surface snapshots across page navigations inside Viz, advancing under Interop 2026 alongside <code>document.activeViewTransition</code> and <code>waitUntil</code> controls.",
  "ch10.d4": "<strong>Speculation Rules prerendering</strong>: Using <code>&lt;script type=\"speculationrules\"&gt;</code> or HTTP headers, pages can ask Chromium to prefetch or fully prerender likely next links in a hidden pipeline—running Parse, Style, LayoutNG, Paint, and initial raster before the user clicks.",
  "ch10.h.claim": "Open frontiers, trials & limits",
  "ch10.c1": "<strong>Prerender budgets & origin trials</strong>: Chromium caps simultaneous background prerenders to protect memory and battery, and continues testing finer activation controls such as the <code>prerender until script</code> origin trial.",
  "ch10.c2": "<strong>Remaining physical ceilings</strong>: Two-phase cross-document view transitions continue refining startup latency, while large image decoding and tile memory pressure on constrained mobile GPUs remain real hardware limits that Graphite's recorder parallelism alone cannot erase.",
  "ch10.model.title": "Model 9 · Ganesh vs. Skia Graphite recorders & Speculation Rules",
  "ch10.model.how": "Switch raster backends (Ganesh vs. Graphite), drag draw-command complexity and parallel Recorder threads, and toggle Speculation Rules prerender hits.",
