  "ch10.say.graphitePrerender": "<strong>Graphite</strong> splits recording across {thr} threads ({rec} ms) and drops <em>{rej}%</em> hidden overdraw via 2D depth testing; with a prerender hit, click-to-paint drops to <em>{nav} ms</em>!",
  "ch10.say.graphiteCold": "<strong>Graphite</strong> cuts recording to {rec} ms and rejects {rej}% overdraw, yet missing prerender still runs the cold pipeline ({nav} ms click-to-paint).",
  "ch10.say.ganeshPrerender": "Even when prerendering trims navigation to {nav} ms, single-threaded <strong>Ganesh</strong> still pays {rec} ms CPU recording plus {ovr}% wasted overdraw.",
  "ch10.say.ganeshCold": "Single-threaded <strong>Ganesh</strong> spends {rec} ms recording with {ovr}% overdraw on top of cold page setup—taking <em>{nav} ms</em> from click to first lit pixel.",
  "ch10.p2": "Switch the backend from legacy single-threaded <code>Ganesh</code> (cold navigation, no 2D depth culling) to <code>Skia Graphite</code> with 3 parallel <code>Recorder</code> threads and a <code>Speculation Rules</code> prerender hit: CPU command-recording work drops from 8.40 ms to 2.20 ms, 2D depth testing rejects 34% of occluded overdraw before fragment shading, and navigation-to-paint latency plunges from 103.4 ms down to 12.8 ms.",
  "ch10.limit": "Every milestone, date, and rollout claim in this chapter reflects <strong>November 2026</strong> public status (Chrome Platform Status, Chromium official blog, and Skia Graphite rollout reports); the model's millisecond and overdraw percentages are parameterized architectural comparisons rather than cross-device hardware benchmarks.",
  "ch11.title": "Wrap-up: when a web page janks, ask three questions first",
  "toc.ch11": "Wrap-up: ask three questions",
  "ch11.law": "You do not need to memorize hundreds of Blink classes. Whenever a page scrolls sluggishly, freezes mid-animation, or spikes GPU memory, open DevTools Performance and ask three questions in order.",
  "ch11.q1": "<strong>Did the frame stall on the Main Thread or in Compositor / GPU?</strong> Look for red-flagged Long Tasks (&gt; 50 ms) or tall yellow/purple Style and Layout bars on Main (chapters 1, 8, 9). If Main sits completely idle while scrolling stutters or flashes grey blocks, check tile rasterization and GPU throughput (chapter 7).",
  "ch11.q2": "<strong>Did a cheap change force Layout, Paint, or Forced Reflow?</strong> Verify animations use compositor-friendly <code>transform</code> or <code>opacity</code> rather than <code>top / left / width / box-shadow</code> (chapters 5, 9), check loops for read-after-write layout thrashing (chapter 4), and inspect heavy stylesheets for universal-bucket tails like <code>.card *</code> (chapter 3).",
  "ch11.q3": "<strong>Did overlapping layers explode VRAM or outrun tiling?</strong> Open the Layers panel: if dozens of cards split into separate textures because an animated badge sits behind them, fix paint order or remove overlap (chapter 6); if fast flings expose checkerboards, keep main-thread commits small so OOP-R and Skewport stay ahead of scroll velocity (chapter 7).",
  "ch11.sum": "In one sentence: Chromium keeps a web page fast by splitting <strong>complex document understanding</strong> (Blink's Main Thread) from <strong>high-frequency frame assembly</strong> (<code>cc</code> and <code>Viz</code>)—your job is simply not to force the wrong half to run at 120 Hz.",
  "ch11.links": "Further reading: <a href=\"../how-threejs-draws-a-scene/\">How Three.js Draws a Scene</a> (how a 3D scene turns objects into WebGL/WebGPU draw calls inside a canvas), <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-gpu-computes/#ch9\">How a GPU Computes</a> (how GPU SMs shade rasterized triangles in 2×2 pixel quads), <a href=\"../../systems/how-pixels-reach-a-screen/#ch1\">How Pixels Reach a Screen</a> (how the display engine scans out the finished framebuffer), and <a href=\"../../systems/how-pixels-reach-a-screen/#ch3\">How Pixels Reach a Screen</a> (why mismatched clocks tear or stutter across double and triple buffering).",
  "footer.sources": "<strong>Sources and internals.</strong> Chromium RenderingNG architecture and source modules (<code>third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/parser/html_document_parser.cc</code>, <code>html_preload_scanner.cc</code>; <code>core/css/selector_checker.cc</code>, <code>selector_filter.h</code>, <code>invalidation/invalidation_set.h</code>; <code>core/layout/block_layout_algorithm.cc</code>, <code>constraint_space.h</code>, <code>physical_fragment.h</code>; <code>core/paint/paint_property_tree_builder.cc</code>; <code>platform/graphics/compositing/paint_artifact_compositor.cc</code>; <code>cc/tiles/tile_manager.cc</code>, <code>cc/trees/layer_tree_host_impl.cc</code>; <code>components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc</code>). File notes and live browser verification live in <code>BRIEF.md</code> and <code>tools/crosscheck.cjs</code> (targeting host Playwright Chromium <code>151.0.7922.34</code>).",
  "footer.models": "<strong>References and November 2026 frontier sources.</strong> Chromium RenderingNG documentation and Life of a Frame series; Chrome Platform Status (<code>chromestatus.com</code>): Scroll-Driven Animations (Chrome 115+ / 133), Cross-Document View Transitions (Chrome 126+), Speculation Rules API; Google Chrome blog \"Introducing Skia Graphite: Chrome's rasterization backend for the future\" (shipped on Apple Silicon starting Chrome 135 in April 2025 and expanded across desktop channels through 2025–2026).",
  "footer.ai": "<strong>About the models.</strong> All nine models in <code>models.js</code> reimplement the core mechanics in pure JavaScript (preload scheduling, Bloom-filtered style invalidation, LayoutNG forced-reflow counting, four-tree matrix and clip evaluation, <code>PaintArtifactCompositor</code> overlap splitting, <code>cc::TileManager</code> skewport tiling, <code>SurfaceAggregator</code> pacing, 120 Hz long-task isolation, and parallel Skia Graphite command recording). Microsecond per-node layout and per-tile raster rates are parameterized to make frame-budget cliffs visible rather than claiming fixed cross-device hardware timings. Written by an AI.",
